## D1. 600 s mixed soak (4 threads, ~70% reads / 30% keyed writes, 8 DBs:
## 6 fleet imports + 2 drill DBs)
- 204,295 reqs (~340/s): 189,554 x200, 14,741 x429 (7.2%), ZERO x500.
- p50 3.1 ms, p99 79.4 ms.
- Service RSS (60 x 10 s samples): min 9.0, median 9.8, max 10.2 MB.
- NOTE on the 429s: the driver used a unique Idempotency-Key per write and
  hit the documented 256-keys-per-principal-per-DB replay cap, not lane
  saturation. Exact proof: soak_ev held exactly 256 rows, burst_ev exactly
  256 soak-era rows (see D2). Client guidance: high-frequency keyed writers
  must reuse keys or expect 429 past 256 live keys.

## D2. Burst (12 threads x keyed INSERTs, one DB, no retry, 300 s)
- Accidental contract proof: 88,982 reqs, ALL 429, zero 500s, p50 14.5 ms.
  Past the replay cap every keyed write sheds fast by design.
- Rerun UNKEYED (pure admission contention, 300 s): 40,828 reqs (~136/s,
  single-DB exclusive guard serializes): 40,826 x200, 2 x429, zero 500s,
  p50 80.3 ms (queueing, as designed), p99 207.9 ms (inside 500 ms timeout).
- Recovery: burst_ev exported (667,648 bytes, sha256
  56a22be9…1744ea97 receipt match), local `integrity_check` ok, 41,082 rows
  = 40,826 burst + 256 soak-keyed, 13 distinct tags. Zero lost acknowledged
  writes. RSS during burst: 10.7–10.8 MB steady (31 samples).
- Time-to-idle: immediate (no backlog — shedding is synchronous).

